---
import PostLayout from "../../../components/PostLayout.astro";
import { Code } from "astro:components";

export const meta = {
    title: "Markdown is actually not _that_ extensible",
    date: new Date(2026, 3, 4),
    description: "Where Markdown's HTML/directive hybrids fall apart",
};
---

<PostLayout meta={meta}>
    <p>
        This came about from a <a
            href="/posts/20260404-hot-takes-from-indiewebclub-in-the-absence-of-tanvi"
            >hot take</a
        > mentioned at <a href="https://blr.indiewebclub.org">IndieWebClub</a>
        by Luna. Sure, markdown is extensible but it is quite bad with extensibility.
    </p><p>
        I used to think you can write HTML in Markdown but it doesn't quite
        work well in that the hybrid of HTML and Markdown are quite bad. For
        example when writing a figure, the figcaption in the below directive
        no longer supports markdown or HTML. So this doesn't quite work for
        adding links to captions or making text italics.
    </p>
    <Code
        lang="mdx"
        code={`:::figure{.full-bleed}
![Alt text!!](./20250425-1.png)
:figcaption[My figcaption works]
:::`}
    /><p>
        This is similarly limiting in Markdoc, which is a super-set of
        Markdown which I use for my website.
    </p>
    <Code
        lang="php"
        code={`{% figure src="./20220202-3.png" alt="" caption="Make this a figcaption." /%}`}
    /><p>
        This is why I want to write all my documents using typst, which has
        a good set of tools for diagrams, layouts, exporting to PDF, and is
        quite fast.
    </p>
</PostLayout>
